## Decisions

### Should ordinary MCP connections stay read-only, with separate consent for clients that need to edit?

This security audit found a verified OAuth consent mismatch. A connection approved by a member receives a token for that member. The iPhone app and laptop tools need that token to make edits; third-party MCP connectors are currently told they can change nothing, although their tokens can call the same write API.

The local reproduction registered a connector with the return address `https://connector.example/callback`, approved it as sample parent Rowan Ashdown, and exchanged the approval for a token. The approval description said `changes: false`. Using only that token, the connector changed Rowan's displayed name to **Changed by read-only connector** through `/api/q`, the shared data-model endpoint. The request returned HTTP 200 and the new name was stored. No production data was touched. A malicious connector could make any edit its approving member is allowed to make; an admin's connection carries their admin authority.

The audit instructions require asking you when a fix needs a decision about who should be allowed to act. This choice determines the permissions connections receive.

- (*) Enforce read-only connections by default
  On the reproduced input, the connector still reads Rowan's data, but its attempt to change Rowan's name receives HTTP 403 and Rowan's name stays unchanged. A client needing edits explicitly requests that authority and the approval page says it can change what Rowan can change. The iPhone app retains its existing edit approval; laptop tools request edit approval too. Existing tokens need to be replaced through sign-in so the new permission is carried in the token. I recommend this because the permission matches what people approve.
- ( ) Disclose full access for every connection
  On the same reproduced input, the name change still receives HTTP 200 and stores **Changed by read-only connector**. Every connector's approval page instead says it can change what Rowan can change, as the iPhone app's page already does. Connections retain their existing authority and tokens; this is simpler, but connecting any third-party MCP client grants it editing authority even though the MCP tools themselves only read.

