### Shall I let MCP clients make updates through the existing write API and make the approval page say so?

Helios MCP currently offers only reading tools, while every approved connection already receives a token that can use Heliosian's write API with the approving person's permissions. Its approval page incorrectly says it can change nothing. Your suggestion adds the useful option I left out: let the MCP client make updates directly.

The verified local example uses sample parent Rowan Ashdown. A connector's token successfully changed Rowan's displayed name to **Changed by read-only connector** through the existing write API. Normal data-model permissions still apply: Rowan can edit their household's profiles, but cannot grant themselves admin rights.

- (*) Let MCP clients make updates
  Add an MCP write tool that uses the same batch writer and permissions as the apps. For example, the client can send `{"batch":[{"set":"per00000000002","cells":{"name_long_override":"Rowan Ashdown"}}]}`; the referenced row is Rowan's sample profile, and `name_long_override` supplies its displayed full name. The tool stores **Rowan Ashdown** when connected as Rowan and refuses edits outside their permissions. Every connection's approval says it can change what you can change. This keeps one permission model and requires no separate read-only token system.
- ( ) Keep ordinary MCP clients read-only
  On the same profile edit, an ordinary MCP connection receives HTTP 403 and the stored name stays unchanged. Clients needing updates must request separate edit consent, carried in their tokens; the iPhone app and laptop tools retain a route to that authority. This requires permission scopes and replacing existing tokens.

I recommend allowing updates through the shared write path, as you suggested. This would extend the audit fix to the MCP tool list as well as correcting the consent statement.

